# Ch368 Systemic Lupus Erythematosus

## Definition, Prevalence, and Epidemiology

## Diagnosis and Classification

## Pathogenesis

## Clinical Manifestations

- **Overview of the multiple manifestations of SLE:** 
![](../img/overview_of_clinical_manifestations_of_sle.png)
- **Non-specific systemic manifstations** - constitutional Sx that occur w/ or w/o single-organ or multi-organ involvement w/ presence of autoAb labelled as the cause of Sx:
- **Musculoskeletal manifestations:**
  - **Myalgia** - various reasons for muscle aches in SLE:
    - <u>Myalgia</u> - non-specific manifestation possibly related to underlying disease activity
    - <u>Inflammatory myopathies</u> - may be seen in lupus, characterised by symmetrical proximal weakness, elevated CK and aldolase levels, w/ characteristic inflammatory infiltrates on Bx
    - <u>Drug-induced myopathy</u> - due to high-dose corticosteroid use, and rarely HCQ
  - **Lupus arthritis** - classically a non-erosive polyarthritis:
    - <u>Site</u> - most commonly florid polyarthritis affecting the wrist, MCPs, PIPs, knees, and elbow joints
    - <u>Other manifestations</u>:
      - **Jaccoud arthropathy** - deformation due to damage of the periarticular ligaments (screening MRI studies suggest possible role of cartilage erosion but much less severe in RA to the point not visible clinically and in XR)
      - **Rhupus** - erosive arthritis due to the co-existence of lupus and RA
  - **Avascular necrosis of the bone** - should be considered for single bone/ joint pain (e.g. shoulder, hip, knee) that is <u>out-of-proportion</u> to the other joints
- **Mucocutaneous manifestations** - 3 major categories of SLE manifestations, where <u>ACLE is highly associated w/ SLE disease activity and visceral organ involvement</u>, while SCLE and chronic cutaneous lupus may occur in absence of systemic disease:
  - **Acute cutaneous lupus erythematosus** (ACLE):
    - <u>Malar rash</u> (classical) - erythematous rash over the cheek of the face w/ sparing of the nasolabial folds, slightly raised due to the photo-sensitivity nature
    - <u>Generalised maculopapular rash</u> - occurs in the sun-exposed areas of the body (e.g. face, limbs)
    - <u>Generalised photosensitivity</u> - rash formation in sun-exposed areas which may or may not be generalised
    - <u>Bullous lupus</u> - rare blistering disease in sun-exposed areas due to subepideral involvement
    - <u>Toxic epidermal necrolysis</u> - widespread blistering and desquamative skin lesions in a similar to the drug-induced disease
  - **Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus** (SCLE) - seen in subgroup of SLE patients demonstrating <u>anti-Ro (SSA) positivity</u>:
    - <u>Annular lesions</u> - migratory, non-scarring flat, red-drimmed, annular lesions
    - <u>Psoriaform lesions</u> - erythematous papules w/ whitish, silvery scaling
  - **Chronic cutaneous lupus:**
    - <u>Discoid lupus</u> (most common) - rough, circular, slightly raised lesions, and are hyperpigmented w/ depigmented atrophic centers and erythematous rim, may result in scarring allopecia
    - <u>Other forms of lupus</u>:
      - Hypertrophic or verrucous lupus
      - Lupus panniculitis
      - Tumid lupus
      - Chilblains lupus
      - Discoid lupus/lichen planus overlap
  - **Other mucocutaneous manifestations** - non-specific features:
    - Non-scarring alopecia
    - Oral and nasal mucosal ulcers (painful or non-painful)
    - Leukocytoclastic and urticarial vasculitdes
- **Raynaud's phenomenon** - only seen in <u>one-third</u> of SLE patients, which is considered atypical
- **Manifestations related to vascular occlusions** - MI and stroke due to general accelerated atherosclerosis and thrombotic nature of anti-phospholipid syndrome:
  - <u>Accelerated atherosclerosis</u> - vascular injury due to excessive type 1 IFN and cellular death predisposes to atherosclerosis
  - <u>Antiphospholid syndrome</u> - characterised by arterial, venous, or small vessel thromboembolic events due to pro-thrombotic properties of the antiphospholids (up to 19% increased risk of stroke compared to general populations)
- **Renal manifestations:**
  - **Glomerulonephritis** (\> 35%) - higher rates and greater severity in those non-White patients, where renal recovery is rare and most frequently progresses to ESRD: 
  ![](../img/ultrastructural_features_of_lupus_nephritis.png)
  - **Other contributary causes to CKD in lupus nephritis:**
    - APS nephropathy
    - Accelerated atherosclerosis
    - Hypertension
    - Treatment-related risk factors (e.g. IFG and HL from protracted course of corticosteroids)
- **Nervous system manifestations** - may be focal or diffuse:
  - <u>Brain fog</u> (most common) - cognitive dysfunction or slowing of thought, so commonly described and attributed to autoAb crossing BBB and inducing neuroinflammation and neuronal damage
  - <u>Headaches</u> - common and severe, and difficult to distinguish from migraines or TTH
  - <u>Acute psychosis or delirium</u> - may occur de novo in the absence of structural abnormalities, but **infectious encephalitis** or **glucocorticoid-induced psychosis** must be r/o
  - <u>Seizures</u> - of focal or generalised type may be seen in neuropsychiatric lupus
  - <u>Transverse myelitis</u> - rare SLE complication due to spinal cord inflammation
- **Serosal manifestations** - serositis and effusions:
  - <u>Pleuritis and pleural effusion</u> - pleuritic chest pain w/ or w/o **exudative pleural effusion** (may be ADA +ve due to presence of mononuclear infiltrates)
  - <u>Pericarditis and pericardial effusion</u>- typically manifests as central chest pains exacerbated by breathing and posture, rarely w/ tamponade effect, often responds well to colchicine, anti-IL-1 drugs, or NSAIDs w/ or w/o glucocorticoids
- **Cardiac and pulmonary manifestations unrelated to serositis:**
  - **Cardiac involvement:**
    - <u>Myocarditis</u> - rare but can manifest as ADHF or arrhythmias
    - <u>Libman-Sack endocarditis</u> - sterile fibrinous endocarditis w/ thromboembolic potential a/w APLS
    - <u>Conduction system disorders</u> - likely as a result of fibrosis from myocarditis or ischaemia from vasculitis, presenting as sick sinus syndrome, heart block
  - **Pulmonary involvement:**
    - <u>Pneumonia</u> - due to immunosuppressive nature
    - <u>Acute pneumonitis</u> - a/w pulmonary infiltrates that cannot be distinguished from infection on imaging
    - <u>Pulmonary embolism</u> - occurs w/ or w/o presence of APS
    - <u>Shrinking lung syndrome</u> - reduced lung volumes and restrictive physiology
    - <u>Pulmonary arterial HTN</u> - typically more inflammatory in nature (cf SSc), results in RHF
    - <u>Others</u> - e.g. diffuse alveolar haemorrhage (capillaritis), interstitial lung disease
- **Haematologic manifestations** - cytopenias often <u>multifactorial</u> and may be a/w 1) disease activity, 2) medication use, or 3) infections:
  - <u>Anaemia</u> (\> 50%) - due to anaemia of chronic illness (1/3 causes), or other causes include iron deficiency anaemia, autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, MAHA (atypical HUS), and medication effects
  - <u>Leukopenia</u> - (50%) - usually as a result of lymphopenia rather than neutropenia, w/ an underlying autoimmune cause
  - <u>Thrombocytopenia</u> - usually mild (\< 150 x 10<sup>9</sup>/L) but in rare cases severe and poses a bleeding tendancy, usually due to underlying immune mechanisms (including APL against platelet Ag)
  - <u>Atypical HUS</u> - condition w/ presence of haemolysis, thrombocytopenia, and microvascular thrombosis +/- brain or other tisue involvement, w/ laboratory testing showing schistocytes, low ADAMTS13 activity and elevated serum LDH levels
- **Gastrointestinal manifestations** - can involev any part of the gastrointestinal tract:
  - <u>Non-specific GI Sx</u> - nasuea, vomiting, diarrhoea, due to mucosal involvement
  - <u>Abdominal pain</u> - non-localising and may be due to 1) lupus peritonitis, 2) lupus enteritis, 3) lupus vasculitis, or 4) lupus vasculitis
  - <u>Mesenteric vasculitis</u> - typically manifests as severe abdominal pain w/ PR bleeding or intestinal perforation
- **Occular manifestations:**
  - <u>Keraconjunctivitis sicca</u> - common Sx complex in SLE **even in the absence of secondary Sjogren's syndrome**
  - <u>Possibly sight threatening complications related to disease activity</u>:
    - Retinal vasculitis
    - Uveitis, and peripheral ulcerative keratitis
    - Scleritis and episcleritis
    - Non-specific conjunctivitis
  - <u>Complications relating to Tx</u>:
    - Bulls-eye retinopathy (related to HCQ dosing \[\> 5 mg/kg/d\], and duration ot Tx \[\> 5y\])
    - Cataracts (related to GC)
    - Acute close-angle glaucoma (related ot GC)

## Laboratory Tests

- **Autoantibodies** - anti-nuclear antibodies (ANA) +/- specific autoantibodies against extractable nuclear antigens (anti-ENA):
  - **ANA** - presence of antigens that cross-react w/ the nucleus in commercially-bought cells (possibly also to the patients cells), and serves as a sign of <u>immune dysregulation</u>:
    - <u>Test properties</u> - 99% of SLE patients will have ANA positivity (-ve results almost always r/o SLE except if anti-Ro +ve)
    - <u>Principles of Dx</u> - only requires "ever-presence of ANA", rather than persistence of ANA:
      - **Titre** - EULAR/ ACR criteria requires presence of ANA titre of \>= 1:80 (although most use a cutoff of 1:160)
      - **Pattern** - various patterns of staining noted but most a/w:
        - Homgenous pattern
        - Speckled pattern
  - **Specific autoantibodies** - usually against 1) components of the nucleosome (most commonly anti-dsDNA), or RNA-binding proteins (RBPs; most commonly anti-Sm): 
  ![](../img/autoantibodies_in_sle_harrisons.png)
    - Anti-Ro is the most common Ab identified in ANA-negative lupus and is a/w subacute lupus erythematosus, refractory photosensitivity, sicca symptoms, and neonatal lupus
    - Anti-histone antibodies is a/w drug-induced lupus
- **Routine bloods** - immunological, renal, and haematologica domains make up the laboratory testing for diagnosis of SLE:
  - **Immunological domains** - autoAb and C3/4:
    - <u>AutoAb</u> - ANA positivity; specific autoAb (e.g. anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm)
    - <u>C3/4</u> - reduced levels w/ increased activity (esp. w/ lupus nephritis)
  - **CBC** - screen for cytopenias, haemolytic anaemias or atypical haemolytic-uraemic syndrome
  - **Renal domains** - RFT, urine dipstick, urine protein quantification:
    - <u>RFT</u> - as baseline eGFR and sCr or to detect acute renal involvement
    - <u>Urine dipstick</u> - for proteinuria and haemoturia
    - <u>Urine micrscopy</u> - for presence of casts in active LN
    - <u>Urine protein quantification</u> - UPCr +/- 24h urine quantification (UPCr of 0.5 prompts renal Bx for active lupus nephritis)
- **Approach to laboratory testing for following disease course of lupus nephritis** - by quarterly testing of immunological, renal, and haematological domain as high disease activity in these domains may be initially asymptomatic

## Management

- **Monitoring of the patient w/ SLE:**
  - <u>Monitoring of renal function</u>
  - <u>Monitoring for disease activity</u>
  - <u>Monitoring of S/E of Tx</u>
- **Therapies for skin and musculoskeletal manifestations:**
  - <u>General measures</u> - **topical sunscreen** and **antimalarials**
  - <u>Additional pharmacological therapies</u> - immunomodulatory and immunosupprssant drugs preferred for steroid sparing effects:
    - Topical agents - topical steroids or CNIs can be utilised
    - Preferred immunomodulatory drugs - azathioprine, leflunomide, MMF, mycophenolic acid
    - Other drugs:
      - Biologics - e.g. belimumab, anifrolumab
      - Other immunomodulatos - MTX (for arthritis), lenalidomide (limited by toxicity but approved for use)
- **Therapies for non-renal SLE:** 
![](../img/harrisons_mx_of_non_renal_sle.png)
- **Antimalarials** (hydroxychloroquine \[HCQ\]) - one of the first drugs approved by US FDA for use in SLE:
  - <u>Indications</u> - for all patients unless contraindicated:
    - First line therapy for isolated mucocutaneous and musculoskeletal manifestations
    - Shown to benefit survival, prevent flares, and decrease risk of renal disease and accelerated atherosclerosis
  - <u>Dosing</u> - optimal dose still under investigation:
    - Dosing up to 5 mg/kg/d
    - Higher doses may confer better disease control
  - <u>S/E</u>:
    - Rash and skin dyspigmentation
    - GI disturbances (N/V/D)
    - Bulls-eye retinopathy (dose-response relationship)
    - QTc prolongation
  - <u>Caution</u> - note HCQ is safe during pregnancy and should be continued:
    - Smoking cessation as smoking interferes with efficacy of antimalarials
    - Avoided in QTc
    - Yearly intervals for retinal evaluations
- **Glucocorticoids** - high-dose chronic use is not advisable and SLE research is aimed at developing Tx algorithms less dependent on glucocorticoid use:
  - <u>Dosing</u> - dependent on organ-specific needs and disease severity:
    - IV pulse MP 125-1000 mg/d for 1-3 days considered for severe disease manifestations
    - PO prednisolone should be tapered to as low as \< 5 mg/d to limit S/E
  - <u>S/E</u> - dose-dependent S/E include:
    - HTN
    - Cushingoid metabolic efefcts
    - Avascular necrosis
    - Osteoporosis
- **Therapies for Mx of renal disease:**
  - <u>Indications for Tx of renal nephritis</u> - considered for all patients w/ Bx-proven:
    - Class III nephritis
    - Class IV nephritis
    - Class V nephritis w/ class III or IV involvement
  - <u>Goals of therapy</u> - EULAR targets \< 10% eGFR from baseline and limitation of proteinuria:
    - 6mo target - 50% reduction of UPCR to 24h urine protein \< 3g/d
    - 12-24mo targets - UPCR \< 0.5-0.7 g/d
  - <u>General adjunctive Tx for renal protection</u>:
    - RAAS blockade - ACEi/ ARBs to decrease HTN, minimise proteinuria
    - SGLT2i - if reduced baseline eGFR for reduce glomerular hyperinfiltration, decreasing proteinuria and BP lowering
    - Warfarin - if evident of APS nephropathy on biopsy
  - <u>Specific regimens for lupus nephritis</u>: 
  ![](../img/tx_of_lupus_nephritis.png)
    - **Initial regimen:**
      - Induction therapy for most w/ steroids (IV or pulse depending on severity) with either MMF or low-dose CYC +/- belimumab
      - High-dose CYC considered for those w/ poor prognostic markers (e.g. RPGN, severe interstitial inflammation, and poor baseline eGFR), but risks ovarian failure
    - **Maintenance thrapy** - tapered dose PO steroid w/ either AZA or MMF (MMF has better renal effect but teratogenic) as steroid sparing agent +/- belimumab
- **Therapies for other organ-based manifestations:**
  - <u>Haematological manifesataitons</u> - lupus-related haemolytic anaemia and thrombocytopaenia treated w/ **rituximab**
  - <u>Serositis</u> - pleural, pericardial, or peritoneal inflammatory Sx respond well on colcicine or additionally NSAIDs
  - <u>Anti-phospholipid syndrome</u> - life-long warfarin (LMWH and aspirin during pregnancy)
  - <u>CNS manifesations</u> - MMF, cyclophosphamide, and rituximab w/ steroids as mainstay of neuropsychiatric lupus and lupus a/w transverse myelitis

## Drug-induced Lupus

## Pregnancy and Reproductive Health

- **Fertility** - factors contributing to subfertility or infertility in SLE patients compared w/ healthy controls:
  - <u>Disease activity</u> - lower measures of ovarian reserve in SLE patients w/ high disease activity possibly due to effects of menstrual and ovulation cycle
  - <u>Rheumatological comorbidities</u> - comorbidiities such as antiphosholipid syndrome may result in subfertility
  - <u>Cyclophosphamide use</u> - use of high-dose cyclophosphamide may result in **premature ovarian insufficiency**, which is partly ameliorated by concurrent use of GnRH agonists w/ cyclophosphamide dosing
- **Contraception and hormone replacement** - must be discussed w/ all women of reproductive age w/ SLE:
  - <u>Estrogen containing compound</u> - should be avoided in moderate-to-severe disease activity, or in the presence of antiphospholipid Ab due to **risk of thromboembolism**
  - <u>Progestin-only contraceptions</u> - as the safe alternative for women with SLE seaking contraceptions (including emergency contraceptions)
- **Pregnancy** - high-risk pregnancies as disease acitivity in pregnancy is unpredictable and disease can flare during any trimester:
  - <u>Adverse pregnancy outcomes in SLE</u>:
    - Pregnancy loss
    - Preterm birth
    - Pre-eclampsia or gestational HTN
    - Intrauterine growth restriction
    - Cesarean section
    - Maternal deaths
  - <u>Predictors of disease flares during pregnancy and peripartum period</u>:
    - High disease activity at conception (most important as avoidable w/ family planning)
  - <u>Risk factors for maternal death in patients w/ SLE</u> - pregnancy should be avoided in:
    - Pulmonary arterial hypertensio
    - Prior arterial thrombosis
    - High disease activity
    - Severe end-organ damage
  - <u>Safe medications for SLE</u> - choice of agents is limited during pregnancy:
    - Pregnancy-compatible immunosuppressants - HCQ, azathioprine, tacrolimus (N.B. MMF, MTX, and other biologics are strongly C/I)
    - Anticoagulation - LMWH for SLE pregnancies w/ APS (warfarin C/I during pregnancy)
- **Neonatal lupus** - a/w strongly w/ SS-A (anti-Ro) which is capable of crossing the placenta into fetal circulation and puts the infant at risk of neonatal lupus:
  - Can manifest as transient cutaneous rash, laboratory abnormalities at birth, or congenital heart block present in utero
  - Screening by fetal echocardiography for congenital heart block esp. w/ high titres of anti-Ro
  - HCQ reduces risk of development of congenital heart block, and fluorinated steroids may be considered for 1st-degree and 2nd-degree heart block detected on fetal echocardiography

## Outomes

- **Difficulties in measurement of SLE outcomes:**
  - Complex given the multisystem nature of the diseas
  - Various aspects of disease require unique treatment approaches and often at differing rates, which make measuring response challenging
- **Modern SLE treatment outcomes:**
  - Improvement of inflammatory type symptoms (type 1 symptoms)
  - Improvement of chronic type symptoms (type 2 symptoms)
  - Improvement of quality of life
  - Prevention of frailty and damage
- **Instruments used in trials for measurements of Tx responsiveness:**
  - Systemic Lupus Erythematosus Responder Index (SRI)
  - British Isles Lupus Assessment Group (BILAG)-based Composite Lupus Assessment (BICLA)
  - Cutaneous Lupus Disease Area and Severity Index (CLASI)
  - SLEDAI-2K instrument
- **Treat-to-target approaches** - using the validated SLEDAI-2K instruments to achieve the following two states w/ minimal use of steroids and stable use of antimalarials, immunosuppressants, and/or biologics:
  - <u>Lupus low disease activity state</u> (LLDAS) -
  - <u>Lupus remission</u> (DORIS) - no disease activity on SLEDAI-2K
- **Why treat-to-target approach:**
  - Shown to be a feasible goal (LLDAS shown to be attainable in up to 80% patients)
  - Sustained LLDAS for \>= 2y a/w significantly less end-organ damage
- **Damage** - organ or structural damage due to 1) persistent sease activity, and 2) use of medications:
  - <u>Complications related to medications independent of disease activity</u> - dose-dependent manner but even low doses of 5 mg prednisolone appears to be a/w damage accrual:
    - Cataracts
    - Fragility fractures
    - Avascular necrosis
    - Diabetes mellitus and other metabolic dysfunction
    - Accelerated atherosclerosis and major adverse cardiovascular events
- **Malignancies** - one meta-analysis suggests an increased risk of overall cancer and cancer-related deaths, particularly 17 site-specific cancers noted:
  - <u>Digestive cancers</u> - esophagus, colon, anus, hepatobilliary, liver, and pancreas
  - <u>Haematological cancers</u> - HL, NHL, leukaemia, multiple myeloma
  - <u>Others</u> - lung, larynx, cervix, vagina/vulva, kidney, bladder, skin, thyroid
